        "root_concepts": "[perception]",
        "section": "cognitive-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Perception - Cognitive Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Perception is not a camera. It is an *inference engine* — the brain generates hypotheses about the external world and tests them against incoming sensory data. Helmholtz (1860s) called it **unconscious inference**. Every modern theory of perception is a variation on this theme."
    },
    {
      "id": "01-PERCEPTION.md#003",
      "path": [
        "01-PERCEPTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:cognitive-science:01-perception, git-history:cognitive-science:01-perception]",
        "canonical_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "concepts": "[perception]",
        "current_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:cognitive-science:perception",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "cognitive-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[perception]",
        "section": "cognitive-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Perception - Cognitive Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  THE PERCEPTION PIPELINE                                         |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                  |\n|  WORLD → SENSE ORGAN → TRANSDUCTION → NEURAL SIGNAL             |\n|                                                                  |\n|   Two competing streams (always running simultaneously):         |\n|                                                                  |\n|   BOTTOM-UP (data-driven)         TOP-DOWN (hypothesis-driven)   |\n|   ────────────────────            ─────────────────────────      |\n|   Raw sensory input               Prior knowledge                |\n|   Physical features               Expectations                   |\n|   Peripheral processing           Context, goals                 |\n|   Mandatory, automatic            Modulates early processing     |\n|   Fast                            Can operate very early         |\n|                                                                  |\n|              ↓                          ↓                        |\n|              └──────────┬──────────────┘                        |\n|                         ↓                                        |\n|              PERCEPT (best-guess interpretation)                 |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-PERCEPTION.md#004",
      "path": [
        "01-PERCEPTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:cognitive-science:01-perception, git-history:cognitive-science:01-perception]",
        "canonical_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "concepts": "[perception]",
        "current_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:cognitive-science:perception",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "cognitive-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[perception]",
        "section": "cognitive-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Perception - Cognitive Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Bottom-up** alone can't work: the same sensory signal is consistent with infinitely many world states (the *inverse optics* problem — you can't uniquely recover a 3D scene from a 2D image without priors). **Top-down** alone can't work: you'd hallucinate constantly. Normal perception integrates both."
    },
    {
