        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Perception - Cognitive Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Why Gestalt principles exist**: They implement regularities about the natural world. Objects tend to have similar texture, move together, form closed contours. These principles are heuristics that work because they exploit statistical structure in natural scenes."
    },
    {
      "id": "01-PERCEPTION.md#021",
      "path": [
        "01-PERCEPTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:cognitive-science:01-perception, git-history:cognitive-science:01-perception]",
        "canonical_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "concepts": "[perception]",
        "current_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:cognitive-science:perception",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "cognitive-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[perception]",
        "section": "cognitive-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Perception - Cognitive Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Marr interpretation**: Gestalt principles operate at the **algorithmic level** — they describe *how* the visual system groups elements. The computational-level explanation is: recover surfaces and objects from image regions by exploiting co-occurrence statistics."
    },
    {
      "id": "01-PERCEPTION.md#022",
