      "text": "## Color Perception — Two Theories, Both Right"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-PERCEPTION.md#024",
      "path": [
        "01-PERCEPTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:cognitive-science:01-perception, git-history:cognitive-science:01-perception]",
        "canonical_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "concepts": "[perception]",
        "current_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:cognitive-science:perception",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "cognitive-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[perception]",
        "section": "cognitive-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Perception - Cognitive Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Young-Helmholtz Trichromacy** (receptor level):\n- Three cone types: L (long, ~560 nm red), M (medium, ~530 nm green), S (short, ~430 nm blue)\n- Any color = weighted sum of L, M, S activations\n- Predicts: color matches, mixing, common forms of color blindness"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-PERCEPTION.md#025",
      "path": [
        "01-PERCEPTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:cognitive-science:01-perception, git-history:cognitive-science:01-perception]",
        "canonical_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "concepts": "[perception]",
        "current_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:cognitive-science:perception",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "cognitive-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[perception]",
        "section": "cognitive-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Perception - Cognitive Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Hering's Opponent-Process Theory** (post-receptor, ganglion cells + LGN):\n```\nThree opponent channels:\n  Red vs Green    (L - M)\n  Blue vs Yellow  (S - [L+M])\n  Black vs White  (overall luminance)\n```\n- Predicts: afterimages (stare at red → green afterimage)\n- Predicts: opponent colors can't coexist (no \"reddish green\")\n- Explains: yellow is perceived as primary even though no \"yellow cone\""
    },
    {
      "id": "01-PERCEPTION.md#026",
      "path": [
        "01-PERCEPTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:cognitive-science:01-perception, git-history:cognitive-science:01-perception]",
        "canonical_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "concepts": "[perception]",
        "current_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:cognitive-science:perception",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "cognitive-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[perception]",
        "section": "cognitive-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Perception - Cognitive Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**The resolution**: Both are correct — at different levels of the hierarchy. Cones implement trichromacy; post-receptor cells compute opponent channels. This is a textbook example of Marr's point that implementational and algorithmic levels can look very different."
    },
    {
