      "text": "## Depth Perception — Cues and Integration"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-PERCEPTION.md#029",
      "path": [
        "01-PERCEPTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:cognitive-science:01-perception, git-history:cognitive-science:01-perception]",
        "canonical_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "concepts": "[perception]",
        "current_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:cognitive-science:perception",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "cognitive-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[perception]",
        "section": "cognitive-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Perception - Cognitive Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nMONOCULAR CUES (one eye sufficient):\n+----------------------------------+\n|  Perspective (parallel lines     |\n|   converge to vanishing point)   |\n|  Occlusion (A blocks B → A near) |\n|  Relative size (farther=smaller) |\n|  Texture gradient                |\n|  Motion parallax (nearer things  |\n|   move faster in visual field)   |\n|  Shading (light-from-above)      |\n|  Aerial perspective (blue haze)  |\n+----------------------------------+"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-PERCEPTION.md#030",
      "path": [
        "01-PERCEPTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:cognitive-science:01-perception, git-history:cognitive-science:01-perception]",
        "canonical_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "concepts": "[perception]",
        "current_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:cognitive-science:perception",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "cognitive-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[perception]",
        "section": "cognitive-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Perception - Cognitive Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "BINOCULAR CUES:\n+----------------------------------+\n|  Stereopsis (binocular           |\n|   disparity — slightly different |\n|   images → retinal disparity →   |\n|   depth; Bela Julesz random-dot  |\n|   stereogram proved this)        |\n|  Convergence (eyes rotate in     |\n|   for near objects — reliable    |\n|   only within ~2 meters)         |\n+----------------------------------+\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-PERCEPTION.md#031",
      "path": [
        "01-PERCEPTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:cognitive-science:01-perception, git-history:cognitive-science:01-perception]",
        "canonical_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "concepts": "[perception]",
        "current_path": "cognitive-science/01-PERCEPTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:cognitive-science:perception",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "cognitive-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[perception]",
        "section": "cognitive-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Perception - Cognitive Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Bayesian cue combination** (Ernst & Banks 2002): depth cues are combined as weighted averages, with weights proportional to their reliability (inverse variance). This is Maximum Likelihood Estimation — the same precision-weighted fusion used in Kalman filters for sensor fusion. Under Gaussian noise, the optimal Bayesian estimate is a precision-weighted (1/sigma^2) average of the individual cue estimates: x_combined = (x1/sigma1^2 + x2/sigma2^2) / (1/sigma1^2 + 1/sigma2^2). The fact that human depth perception matches this formula quantitatively is one of the strongest pieces of evidence for the Bayesian brain hypothesis."
    }
